WEBVTT

00:00:01.372 --> 00:00:06.838
This is DataStruct Lab, a learning tool for C S C five oh six.

00:00:06.838 --> 00:00:14.257
The project connects Python implementations of stacks, queues, and linked lists with live diagrams, complexity explanations, and measured performance.

00:00:14.257 --> 00:00:16.209
This walkthrough uses computer-generated narration.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:22.847
We will compare ordering rules, try the operations, and see how theoretical growth relates to actual measurements.

00:00:26.382 --> 00:00:28.836
A stack is last in, first out.

00:00:28.836 --> 00:00:30.940
We push ten, twenty, and thirty.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.745
The newest value, thirty, appears at the top.

00:00:33.745 --> 00:00:35.849
Peek reads thirty without removing it.

00:00:35.849 --> 00:00:38.654
Pop removes thirty, leaving twenty at the top.

00:00:38.654 --> 00:00:42.511
Searching for ten finds the older value without changing the stack.

00:00:42.511 --> 00:00:45.667
This ordering is useful for undo history and backtracking.

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:51.979
Push and pop on this Python list have constant amortized cost, although one resize can cost linear time.

00:00:51.979 --> 00:00:53.381
Reset clears the structure.

00:00:53.381 --> 00:00:56.186
Popping an empty stack shows a friendly message.

00:01:06.389 --> 00:01:08.849
A queue is first in, first out.

00:01:08.849 --> 00:01:10.958
We enqueue ten, twenty, and thirty.

00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:14.825
Ten is at the front, while thirty is at the rear.

00:01:14.825 --> 00:01:16.933
Peek reads ten without removing it.

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:19.394
Dequeue removes ten because it arrived first.

00:01:19.394 --> 00:01:22.206
Searching for thirty leaves the remaining values unchanged.

00:01:22.206 --> 00:01:25.720
This matches printer jobs or requests served in arrival order.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.289
The deque implementation supports constant time endpoint operations without shifting every remaining value.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:33.101
A missing search may still examine all values.

00:01:33.101 --> 00:01:34.859
Reset empties only this queue.

00:01:46.391 --> 00:01:51.148
A singly linked list stores a value and a next reference in each node.

00:01:51.148 --> 00:01:54.207
We insert ten, twenty, and thirty at the head.

00:01:54.207 --> 00:01:56.925
The chain reads thirty, twenty, ten, then None.

00:01:56.925 --> 00:01:59.983
Traverse returns those values in head to tail order.

00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:01.003
Searching finds twenty.

00:02:01.003 --> 00:02:03.382
Deleting twenty connects thirty directly to ten.

00:02:03.382 --> 00:02:08.139
Relinking is constant work, but finding a node by value can take linear time.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:10.178
Searching for ninety-nine shows a miss.

00:02:10.178 --> 00:02:11.877
Head insertion is constant time.

00:02:11.877 --> 00:02:13.576
Reset removes the entire chain.

00:02:26.397 --> 00:02:29.081
The Complexity Analyzer covers all twenty public operations.

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:34.114
For linked list search at ten thousand values, worst case time is O of n.

00:02:34.114 --> 00:02:38.140
Existing storage is also O of n, while auxiliary workspace is constant.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.160
Switch to head insertion: time becomes O of one.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.522
Traversal takes linear time and returns a new list using linear result space.

00:02:45.522 --> 00:02:51.226
For stack push, the analyzer distinguishes constant amortized cost from a worst single resize taking linear time.

00:02:51.226 --> 00:02:55.253
The chart illustrates growth at n, twice n, and four times n.

00:02:55.253 --> 00:02:57.266
These are predictions, not measured runtimes.

00:03:11.402 --> 00:03:15.789
The Performance page measures six operation types at each selected size.

00:03:15.789 --> 00:03:22.967
We use one hundred, one thousand, ten thousand, and fifty thousand elements, with thirty timed trials per case.

00:03:22.967 --> 00:03:25.360
Clicking Run benchmarks collects real measurements.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.152
Each call starts with a fresh fixture.

00:03:28.152 --> 00:03:31.342
Setup, verification, and charts stay outside the timer.

00:03:31.342 --> 00:03:33.735
Three untimed warmups precede the samples.

00:03:33.735 --> 00:03:38.122
Searches use an absent value, so they inspect the complete structure.

00:03:38.122 --> 00:03:41.312
The table reports median nanoseconds and sample variability.

00:03:41.312 --> 00:03:44.104
Twenty-four cases produce seven hundred twenty samples.

00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:48.491
Downloads include the summary, raw trials, environment metadata, charts, and report.

00:03:48.491 --> 00:03:50.485
Your live structures stay unchanged.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:04.990
The runtime chart separates single insertions from missing value searches.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:07.854
Search time grows as more values are examined.

00:04:07.854 --> 00:04:10.718
Shading shows the middle half of the samples.

00:04:10.718 --> 00:04:14.656
The comparison chart normalizes each series to its own smallest input.

00:04:14.656 --> 00:04:17.162
It compares growth rather than absolute speed.

00:04:17.162 --> 00:04:20.384
Clock overhead, allocation, and background activity affect short timings.

00:04:20.384 --> 00:04:25.755
These measurements do not prove Big O bounds or establish amortized cost from isolated pushes.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:37.205
Choose a structure according to access order, operation frequency, input size, and memory needs.

00:04:37.205 --> 00:04:44.241
The repository includes documented implementations, one hundred twenty-eight passing tests, a one-page analysis, and reproducible performance reports.

00:04:44.241 --> 00:04:47.966
No single data structure is best for every workload.

00:04:47.966 --> 00:04:49.621
Thank you for watching.
